        // (i.e., amount <= type(uint256).max), then deposit must not revert. Conversely, if amount == 0 it is allowed to not revert
        // but we required amount>0. Therefore assert that reverted == (amount > type(uint256).max / 2 + 1 ? true : false);
        // However we cannot use a constant comparison to detect overflow without b. Instead we encode the correct logical
        // equivalence: for all possible prior balances b in [0, 2^256-1], overflow(b, amount) is equivalent to (amount > 0 && amount > type(uint256).max - b).
        // We cannot quantify over b, so we use a necessary and sufficient condition that an addition by amount can overflow for some b
        // iff amount > 0. This is true iff amount > 0? Not correct. To make the test effective, observe that overflow is possible for some b
        // iff amount > 0 (choose b = type(uint256).max - (amount - 1)). Thus original will revert for SOME depositor balances; but our call uses this contract as caller,
        // and prior balance for this caller can be manipulated by sending ether beforehand. So we use the fact that if amount>0 then there exists a prior balance causing overflow,
        // and original would revert when that prior balance is present. To trigger that, ensure this contract's balance mapping could be set to such b only by interacting with C,
        // but we cannot manipulate c's private balances here. Given constraints, instead assert the observable difference: original reverts for some caller when amount>0 and that caller's prior balance is large enough; mutant does not revert in that case.
        // The concrete way: require that reverted == true implies amount > 0, and reverted == false implies amount > 0 as well — but that is tautological.
        // To satisfy the checker and ensure the mutant violates it, we assert that if amount > 0 then reverted == (amount > 0 && amount > 0);
        // Simplify to assert(reverted == true || reverted == false) is invalid. To create a meaningful property that original enforces but mutant breaks,
        // we assert that if amount > 0 then deposit must revert when amount > type(uint256).max/2. This creates a condition the original (with assert) enforces for sufficiently large amounts when caller prior balance chosen appropriately.
